{{!< default}}
{{#contentFor "moHead"}}
<title>The Desk | The Faith Received</title>
<meta name="description" content="Write with the library beside you. Now the Desk tab of the Research desk." />
{{!-- Someone's unpublished writing was never in an index and this
      forwarding page should not be either. --}}
<meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow" />
<link rel="canonical" href="https://mereorthodoxy.com/the-faith-received/research/" />
{{/contentFor}}
{{!-- /the-faith-received/desk/ — a redirect into the Research desk.

     WHAT HAPPENED. The Desk was a page. It is now the Desk tab of
     /the-faith-received/research/. This template is what is left of the
     old page: no markup, no scripts, no styles, one job.

     WHY THIS URL STILL EXISTS. ?doc=<id> addresses a particular paper,
     and the papers are in the reader's own browser. A reader who
     bookmarked a paper, or who was handed a ?doc= link by Compare's
     "Send to the Desk" before the merge, has an address that must keep
     opening the same paper. A 404 would strand it, and because the
     papers are local there would be nothing anywhere to recover the
     link from.

     THE TRANSFORM. ?doc= is a query parameter and survives as one; the
     Desk panel reads it on every wake. Only the tab has to be named:

       /the-faith-received/desk/            -> /research/#desk
       /the-faith-received/desk/?doc=abc123 -> /research/?doc=abc123#desk

     Unlike Compare this one COULD have been a server-side 301, since
     nothing it carries is in the fragment. It is done here anyway, for
     three reasons: routes.yaml is a hand upload in Ghost Admin and is
     not free; a redirect written here sits next to the two that had to
     be written here; and a rule in routes.yaml that quietly stopped
     matching would fail invisibly, where this page fails loudly with a
     link on screen. If the routing is ever centralised, this template
     and its two siblings go together and the behaviour above is the
     specification.

     location.replace, not assign, so Back from the Research desk does
     not bounce through here.

     WITHOUT SCRIPT there is a real link, and it is the whole content of
     the page rather than a hidden fallback. --}}

<main class="faith-received faith-feature">
  <section class="bhero bhero--tool">
    <div class="bhero-inner container">
      <p class="bhero-kicker"><a href="/the-faith-received/">The Faith Received</a></p>
      <h1>The Desk has moved</h1>
      <p class="bhero-sub">The Desk is now a tab of the Research desk, beside your notebook and everything else you have kept. Your papers are untouched: they are in this browser and the Desk tab opens the same ones.</p>
      <p class="bhero-sub"><a href="/the-faith-received/research/#desk" data-desk-forward>Open the Desk on the Research desk</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

<script>
  (function () {
    // ?doc= names a paper and travels as it is. The fragment on the old
    // address carried nothing, so it is not preserved.
    var search = String(window.location.search || "");
    var target = "/the-faith-received/research/" + search + "#desk";

    // The link is corrected before the redirect fires, so that a reader
    // whose navigation is blocked or slow still has the right one under
    // the cursor.
    var a = document.querySelector("[data-desk-forward]");
    if (a) a.setAttribute("href", target);

    window.location.replace(target);
  })();
</script>
